INT. FLORIST'S WALK-IN COOLER - NIGHT

Buckets of lilies. Roses in sleeves. Cold fog off the compressor. A single caged bulb.

DETECTIVE IMELDA STROUD, 40s, gloves, trench coat, closes the door and lets it click. She leans on a shelf of carnations.

KARINA VOLKOVA, 30s, florist, apron stained green, stands rigid with a pair of pruning shears in both hands.

IMELDA
Put those down.

KARINA
No.

IMELDA
Karina.

KARINA
You said you were alone.

IMELDA
I am alone.

KARINA
You said you'd tell no one.

IMELDA
I haven't.

Karina's eyes flick to the door. To the shears. Back.

KARINA
He's going to find out.

IMELDA
Who?

KARINA
You know who.

IMELDA
Say his name.

Karina shakes her head. Her knuckles whiten on the handles.

IMELDA (CONT'D)
Then say what he did.

KARINA
He bought the van. He bought the van, and the lilies, and the church.

IMELDA
Which church?

KARINA
St. Anselm's. He bought the whole front pew.

Imelda takes a step. Karina lifts the shears. Imelda stops.

IMELDA
You think I'm here to arrest you.

KARINA
Aren't you?

IMELDA
No.

KARINA
Then why the gloves?

IMELDA
It's twenty degrees in here.

A pause. A drip. The compressor hums.

KARINA
(small)
Oh.

Karina lowers the shears an inch. Imelda holds out a hand, palm up.

IMELDA
Give them to me.

KARINA
No.

IMELDA
I'll give them back.

KARINA
You won't.

IMELDA
I will. When this is over.

Karina laughs, a short bitter sound.

KARINA
It's never over.

IMELDA
It's over when you testify.

KARINA
He has a brother.

IMELDA
I know about the brother.

KARINA
He has a cousin on the force.

IMELDA
I know about the cousin.

KARINA
Do you know about the judge?

Imelda goes still.

IMELDA
No.

KARINA
Judge Morales buys flowers every Sunday. Lilies. Always lilies.

Imelda exhales a long white plume. Takes off one glove.

IMELDA
Thank you.

She extends her bare hand again. Karina studies it. The shears slip a little.

KARINA
He'll know it was me.

IMELDA
Not if you walk out of here with a bouquet and a smile.

KARINA
I don't smile.

IMELDA
You'll learn.

Karina drops the shears into a bucket of water. They sink. She wipes her palms on her apron, trembling.

KARINA
(whisper)
I'm so tired.

IMELDA
I know.

KARINA
Do you?

IMELDA
My sister ran a shop like this.

KARINA
What happened?

IMELDA
She stopped being afraid.

KARINA
And?

IMELDA
She sleeps now.

Karina lets out a ragged breath. A smile, almost. She sits on an overturned crate.

Imelda sits on another. Their knees nearly touch.

KARINA
Can I have a cigarette?

IMELDA
No smoking near the lilies.

KARINA
Right. Sorry.

IMELDA
Take this instead.

She pulls a peppermint from her pocket. Karina unwraps it. Puts it in her mouth.

They sit like that. The fog drifts. The compressor cycles off. Silence settles, soft as pollen.

KARINA
Tomorrow?

IMELDA
Tomorrow.

Karina nods once. Pulls a single white lily from a bucket. Hands it over.

KARINA
For the front pew.
